WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.670
No island has a better record of how life arrives on new land.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.217
The first plant, sea rocket, was found in 1965, its seeds carried by the sea.

00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:16.490
In the first decade, shore plants with big, well-stocked seeds took hold on the bare sand.

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.697
Then, from 1975 to 1985, colonisation stalled:

00:00:19.697 --> 00:00:23.879
the barren ash and lava could not support most new arrivals.

00:00:24.979 --> 00:00:26.241
Then the gulls came.

00:00:26.241 --> 00:00:29.332
From about 1985, gulls began breeding on Surtsey.

00:00:29.332 --> 00:00:34.695
Their droppings added about forty-seven kilograms of nitrogen per hectare every year,

00:00:34.695 --> 00:00:38.922
around forty times the natural input, and their guts carried seeds.

00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:44.046
A second wave of colonisation followed, centred on the gull colony,

00:00:44.046 --> 00:00:45.788
which became dense grassland.

00:00:45.788 --> 00:00:48.491
Species numbers peaked at sixty-four in 2007.

00:00:48.491 --> 00:00:52.515
Since then, competitive grasses and erosion have caused a net loss.

00:00:53.615 --> 00:00:59.091
By 2013, sixty-nine plant species had been recorded on Surtsey, fifty-nine were present,

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:01.518
and thirty-nine had viable populations.

00:01:01.518 --> 00:01:04.505
About three quarters of them arrived with birds.
